# ISSUE-NEXT: every three hours, every open issue in viff's tracker carries a proposal for its
# next step.
#
# A _proposal_ is one entity per issue, `repos.viff.git-issue.next-proposal.<id>`. It says who acts
# next and what they do, as the label the issue should carry:
#
#     viff-next=<who>:<step>
#         who   viff | world
#         step  analyze | design | plan | build | push-build | report-build
#
# `viff` means viff proposes to take the step itself; `world` means it waits on somebody else.
#
# THIS IS THE STUB. It writes the proposal and its label and nothing more: every issue it has
# not seen before is proposed `viff:analyze`, and no task runs, so it costs no tokens. Two
# things come after it, and both read what this writes:
#
#   - the git-issue io's out half, which puts the proposal's label on the issue;
#   - the processing task, one viff-runner checkout per sweep, which takes each issue that
#     changed to its proposed step and writes the step after it.
#
# ── A SWEEP, NOT A REACTION ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The unit is the schedule's tick. An issue that moves does not wake anything: the sweep reads
# the issues in its ACTIONS, and only a condition's reads wake a rule. So a burst of comments
# costs nothing until the next tick, installing over a backlog costs one sweep, and the work a
# tick causes is bounded by the open issues rather than by the activity on them.
#
#     viff schedule tick sweep --at repos.viff.git-issue --due <instant>     # a sweep now
#
# The sweep record (`repos.viff.git-issue.sweep`) is what carries the open set from the tick to
# the fan-out: a fan-out walks a list a reference names, and a path read is not a reference.
at: repos.viff.git-issue
reads: [git-issue, mirror.schedule]
schedules:
- name: sweep
  every: 3h
strata:
# The issues, as the git-issue source reflects them. Level 0: this package never writes there.
- name: issues
  level: 0
  match:
    path: "git-issue"
# The sweep's own ticks, which the schedule writes. Above the issues, because the sweep reads
# them: a derive reads at or below the level of its rule.
- name: ticks
  level: 5
  match:
    path: 'mirror.schedule."repos.viff.git-issue:sweep".tick'
- name: sweep
  level: 10
  match:
    path: "repos.viff.git-issue.sweep"
- name: proposals
  level: 20
  match:
    path: "repos.viff.git-issue.next-proposal"
rules:
# 1. At each tick, the open issues are listed onto the sweep record.
#
# A rule about a tick fires once per tick and needs no latch: only the next tick replaces it.
# The list is ids only. The issues' facts are read per issue in rule 2, and a list of whole
# payloads would copy every body and discussion into this record every three hours.
- name: sweep
  stratum: ticks
  condition:
    subject:
      path: 'mirror.schedule."repos.viff.git-issue:sweep".tick.*'
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repos.viff.git-issue"
      id: sweep
      value:
        due: .[subject.value.due]
        open: !list-paths
          at: "git-issue.viff.issue.*"
          value:
            status: open
          path: id
# 2. …and each open issue gets its proposal record, addressed by the issue's id.
#
# The derive owns the issue's facts and nothing else, so it rewrites only what the issue says
# about itself. An issue that did not move since the last sweep derives the same bytes, which is
# no commit; one that moved gets its new `updatedAt`, which is how the processing task will tell
# what changed. The record of an issue that closes stays as it was.
#
# The rule wakes only when the sweep record changes, at a tick: its condition reads nothing
# beyond its subject.
- name: propose
  stratum: sweep
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repos.viff.git-issue.sweep"
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repos.viff.git-issue.next-proposal"
      value: !each
        over: .[subject.value.open]
        id: .[item]
        value:
          issue: .[item]
          title: !get-path
            at: [git-issue, viff, issue, .[item]]
            path: title
          updatedAt: !get-path
            at: [git-issue, viff, issue, .[item]]
            path: updatedAt
# 3. A proposal record with no proposal gets the stub: viff analyzes it next.
#
# A separate rule, latched on the absence of `next`, so that the derive above never writes over
# a proposal. Whatever writes the next one, the processing task or a person, owns `next` and
# `label` from then on.
- name: stub
  stratum: proposals
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repos.viff.git-issue.next-proposal.*"
      value: !not.has-path next
  action:
    patch:
      next: "viff:analyze"
      label: "viff-next=viff:analyze"
